is.\nTurn 72. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen switches back to the blank whiteboard.\nTutor writes \"3/4 x 3/1\" on the whiteboard.\nTurn 72. TUTOR: Three over four times three is the same thing as doing three over four times three over one.\nTurn 73. TUTOR: So what's the answer?\nTurn 74. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 6 seconds] Tutor writes an equals sign after the problem.\nTurn 74. TUTOR: You would do three times three over four times one. So what does it equal?\nTurn 75. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 13 seconds]\nTurn 75. STUDENT: So, four times one equals four and then three times three equals nine.\nTurn 76. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 11 seconds]\nTurn 76. STUDENT: Four nine.\nTurn 77. STUDENT: Three plus four.\nTurn 78. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 78. TUTOR: Nine, yes.\nTurn 79. STUDENT: Four.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
